# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 2: Person Description
#
# The WIEN task for the bus-stop photo of sub-lesson 1: who is present, and 1–2 of
# them described visually. The examiner's second prompt on the picture task is
# almost always "pick one person and describe them" — clothes, what they are
# doing, roughly how old they look — so this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: an older man standing near the shelter, in a
# beige coat and dark trousers, with a black cap, standing calmly and waiting; and
# in front of him, closer to the camera, a younger man standing with a backpack at
# his feet, in a grey pullover and blue jeans.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 — no @question prompts, no first-person opinion.
# A depicted person's visible state (`Hie gesäit roueg aus`) is observation and is
# wanted: the older man's posture and expression are checkable against the photo.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme, with the guard that appearance
# descriptions make easy to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on
# feminine nouns (`eng schwaarz Kap`), where Luxembourgish leaves the adjective
# uninflected. Masculine and neuter attributives need declension the learner has
# not met, so those are stated predicatively instead (`Säi Mantel ass beige.`) —
# except inside `@fill` frames, where a declined attributive may sit in the FIXED
# frame text (never in the blank itself), the same escape hatch groussgaass/
# chreschtmaart use for `en normalen Dag`.
#
# Words sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Leit, d'Bild, gesinn, lénks, riets, …) are
# deliberately NOT repeated — the theme's pass-gate makes 01 a prerequisite for
# 02, and stat keys are shared across the app. Overlap with the other picture
# themes (de Mann, jonk, gro) is accepted: themes are independent and all open, so
# a learner may reach this one first.
#
# Ten @fill blocks carry this file's share of the answering-pattern drill: the
# split `gesäit … aus` state frame (used twice, for opposite subjects), the
# `hunn … un` clothing frame for both a masculine and a feminine garment, a
# checkable-feature opener in place of the usual side opener, two relative-clause
# shapes (singular and plural, so the learner sees the verb agreement contrast),
# a numeral pair, a possessive appearance frame, and an additive garment-colour
# frame — most of the person-description library, each reused with a pairing or
# blank no earlier picture theme has taught for THIS photo's people.

@lesson P4.02 "Busarrêt: Person Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/busarret/img/busarret.webp"
@image-alt "The same bus-stop photo, seen for the people in it: an older man in a beige coat and black cap standing calmly near the shelter, and in front of him a younger man in a grey pullover and blue jeans standing with a backpack at his feet."

# --- People ---
@word de Mann = the man
@word d'Männer = the men
@word eeler = older
@word jonk = young

# --- Clothes ---
@word d'Kap = the cap
@word de Mantel = the coat
@word de Pullover = the pullover
@word d'Box = the trousers
@word d'Jeans = the jeans

# --- Colours ---
@word schwaarz = black
@word beige = beige
@word gro = grey

# --- What they are doing ---
@word hunn = to have
@word ongeféier = approximately

# --- Describing them ---
@word roueg = calm
@word gedëlleg = patient
@word midd = tired

@sentence
@lu Zwee Männer stinn um Busarrêt.
@en Two men are standing at the bus stop.
@distractor-lu dräi
@distractor-lu Fraen
@distractor-en three
@distractor-en women

@sentence
@lu Den eelere Mann huet eng schwaarz Kap un.
@en The older man is wearing a black cap.
@distractor-lu gro
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en grey
@distractor-en trousers

@sentence
@lu Säi Mantel ass beige.
@en His coat is beige.
@distractor-lu schwaarz
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-en black
@distractor-en cap

@sentence
@lu Hie steet gedëlleg um Busarrêt.
@en He stands patiently at the bus stop.
@distractor-lu leeft
@distractor-lu sëtzt
@distractor-en runs
@distractor-en sits

@sentence
@lu Säi Pullover ass gro.
@en His pullover is grey.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en trousers

@sentence
@lu Hien huet eng blo Jeans un.
@en He is wearing blue jeans.
@distractor-lu schwaarz
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-en black
@distractor-en coat

@sentence
@lu Hien huet e Rucksak bei sech.
@en He has a backpack with him.
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en cap
@distractor-en trousers

@sentence
@lu Den eelere Mann ass ongeféier siwwenzeg Joer al.
@en The older man is approximately seventy years old.
@distractor-lu sechzeg
@distractor-lu zéng
@distractor-en sixty
@distractor-en ten

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The visible-state frame with the split `gesäit … aus`. The verb bracket is the
# pattern being taught, so it stays in the frame and the adjective is the blank.
# Both distractors are states nothing in the photo supports for this man.
@fill
@lu Den eelere Mann gesäit [roueg] aus.
@en The older man looks [calm].
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-lu traureg
@distractor-en tired
@distractor-en sad

# The `hunn … un` clothing frame for a masculine garment — the separable `un`
# sits at the end, an order the learner reads rather than builds. `e` is fixed in
# the frame and masculine, so feminine `Kap` and `Box` are ruled out on grammar
# alone (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Den eelere Mann huet e [Mantel] un.
@en The older man is wearing a [coat].
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en cap
@distractor-en trousers

# The same clothing frame with a feminine garment and determiner, so the
# opposite gender rules out `Mantel` this time — R4 doing the work from the other
# side.
@fill
@lu Den eelere Mann huet eng [Kap] un.
@en The older man is wearing a [cap].
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-lu Rucksak
@distractor-en coat
@distractor-en backpack

# A checkable-feature opener, in place of the usual side opener — identifying the
# person by what he is wearing rather than by position. `de` stays fixed and
# masculine, ruling out neuter `Meedchen`/`Kand` on grammar as well as on the
# photo.
@fill
@lu Ech beschreiwen de [Mann] mat der Kap.
@en I am describing the [man] with the cap.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en child

# The relative-clause frame, singular. `deen … waart` is verb-final, so the
# learner reads the order instead of assembling it. Blank is the verb: a noun
# cannot fill a verb slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn e Mann, deen um Busarrêt [waart].
@en I see a man who [is waiting] at the bus stop.
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-lu Rucksak
@distractor-en cap
@distractor-en backpack

# The same relative-clause frame, plural — `déi … waarden` contrasts the singular
# verb form above, so the pair together drills the agreement rather than just the
# word order.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn zwee Männer, déi um Busarrêt [waarden].
@en I see two men who [are waiting] at the bus stop.
@distractor-lu Kap
@distractor-lu Rucksak
@distractor-en cap
@distractor-en backpack

# The numeral-plus-noun pair, mirrored from chreschtmaart with a different
# number and noun. `Beim Busarrêt sinn et` puts the finite verb before both
# blanks, so the numeral tile is the word directly in front of the noun blank
# rather than `sinn` — `dräi` does not end in `-n`, which is what keeps the frame
# clear of the Eifeler Regel (R5). Different word classes in the two blanks
# (numeral, then plural noun), so they cannot swap (R6).
@fill
@lu Beim Busarrêt sinn et [dräi] [Fraen].
@en At the bus stop there are [three] [women].
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-lu Männer
@distractor-en children
@distractor-en men

# The possessive appearance frame — `Säi … ass …` recurs for any person, here
# applied to the older man's coat colour. One blank on purpose: a two-blank
# version would join two predicative adjectives with no forced order, an R6
# violation.
@fill
@lu Säi Mantel ass [beige] a hie huet eng Kap un.
@en His coat is [beige] and he is wearing a cap.
@distractor-lu schwaarz
@distractor-lu gro
@distractor-en black
@distractor-en grey

# The visible-state frame reused for the younger man, with a different checkable
# feature than the older man's calm — his age rather than his mood, which the
# photo's two men make a natural contrast pair.
@fill
@lu De Mann mat dem Rucksak gesäit [jonk] aus.
@en The man with the backpack looks [young].
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en tired
